---
title: "The Next Frontier Isn't Better Text. It's Better Problems."
date: "2025-12-27"
author: "Jason Crittenden; Founder, Shadow Dynamic Systems LLC"
image: "assets/assembly-hall.webp"
---

**Asankhaya Sharma** just pulled off something that deserves some celebratory remarks — published on his Hugging Face blog:

> **The 1 Billion Token Challenge: Finding the Perfect Pre-Training Mix**
> [https://huggingface.co/blog/codelion/optimal-dataset-mixing](https://huggingface.co/blog/codelion/optimal-dataset-mixing)

Using a 70M-parameter GPT-2 variant and only **1 billion tokens**—about one-tenth the GPT-2 training data—he ran more than fifty controlled experiments to isolate the *actual* effects of dataset composition.

The result? A deceptively simple static mixture:

* **50%** finePDFs (high-quality textbook PDFs)
* **30%** DCLM-baseline (filtered web text)
* **20%** FineWeb-Edu (curated educational sources)

That mix trained a small model to within a rounding error of GPT-2 while using dramatically less data and compute. It’s one of the few recent results that’s both **accessible** *and* **substantial**.

At Shadow Dynamic Systems, this is exactly the kind of work we celebrate:
**thoughtful, empirical, replicable experimentation over hype.**

But it also brings a deeper truth into sharp focus:

---

## **Understanding Prose ≠ Solving Problems**

Perfecting dataset mixes is essential for building **prose-understanding** models.

But as these results demonstrate, understanding prose is not the same as **reasoning**, **planning**, or **problem-solving**. 

A textbook can teach terminology.
A web crawl can teach token patterns.

But **problem-solving emerges outside language.**

It arises from:

* uncertainty
* ambiguity
* constraints
* failed attempts
* corrections
* replanning
* sensory variation
* adversarial noise
* physical reality
* consequences

None of these forces exist in a PDF.

You don’t learn navigation from reading maps.
You learn navigation from getting lost—and finding your way back.

You don’t learn triage from reading medical manuals.
You learn triage when seconds matter, information is incomplete, and consequence clamps around your chest like an iron vise.

And no model—no matter how beautifully trained—will ever learn **true reasoning** from text alone.

**Blobs of text will never be enough.**

---

## **What the Field Needs Next**

Sharma’s work shows how far we can push models by giving them the *right words*.
The next step is giving them the **right problems**.

### **1. Problem-Space Training**

Models must interact with iterative, consequence-bearing tasks where failure drives adaptation.

### **2. Realistic State Transitions**

Training must reflect the entropy of reality: noise, partial data, conflicting signals, evolving scenes.

### **3. Agentic Feedback Loops**

Models need to plan, test, repair, revise, and evaluate their own actions—rather than emit the “most likely next token.”

### **4. Purposeful Memory**

Not just long context, but memory organized around relevance, attention, and goals.

### **5. A Curriculum of Real Challenges**

Benchmarks are static puzzles.
Real intelligence emerges in environments where actions reshape the world.

---

## **Where This Leaves Us**

The dataset-mix breakthrough is genuinely worth celebrating.
It proves that smart curation can multiply the capability of small models far beyond expectations.

But the next leap—the one that matters for *actual intelligence*—requires something text cannot provide:

**experience.**

Experience with failure.
Experience with ambiguity.
Experience with problems that cannot be memorized.
Experience where each attempt updates the internal algorithm.

Only problem-solving can teach problem-solving.

And when training moves beyond static corpora and into structured, interactive cognitive development, models will stop being language engines and start becoming **agents**.

Sharma’s work gives us better ingredients.
Now comes the part where models learn to *cook.*

